[{:seon.task/name "prove-runs-what-a-change-reaches"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner 2026-10-22: \"why don't we have a comprehensive policy where we are only running tests that have changed?\" (AGENTS.md section 5: results are reused by input digest, unchanged inputs are never run again; section 9: re-running everything on every change). Design: notes/fork-test-merge-design-2026-10-22.md section 2 and its build step 2. Before: three selections (`bin/test --reach` with its own serial runner, not the default; an agent's `test-vars`, proofs plus `reaching-tests` of `changed-vars`, refs only and never reused; `bin/check` running every step). v1 (~/src/seon seon.test.cache) keyed a result by its reach digest; the field's way (Bazel, Nix, bors) keys an action by the digest of every input. What exists now, one of each: the selection `seon.proofs/plan` (a key runs exactly when no green is stored at its reach digest; with a base, an agent's branch against main at its merge base, it selects the proofs and the moved digests; without, the proofs and every key with no green); the digests `seon.program/reach-digests` (tests by default, bin/check steps by their seon.check functions: `seon.proofs/check-steps`, the one list of the steps, which bin/check reads from `./seon steps`; `seon.proofs/step-digests` names a step whose function is gone as missing, red); the runner `seon.proofs/run-tests!` (bin/test's shards and an agent's test fork; each assertion, start, result and red output told through note!); the results directory `:seon.config.source/test-results-dir` (`seon.proofs/greens`, `seon.proofs/store!` through `seon.fx/write-file`, its answer read). Front doors: `bin/prove` for worktree sessions and root (`bin/test` and `bin/check` without arguments are its halves; `bin/check --steps DIR` runs the selected steps through bin/check's own bodies); `seon.agent/test-plan` for an agent's run (the turn's observe, the reconciler's :tests, the merge gate, `(my.agent/prove)`). Full runs are explicit: `bin/test --all`, `bin/check 100`. A merge runs bin/prove on the merged tree (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches). Deleted: `bin/test --reach` and test-runner's plan, run-vars!, reach-main and green?; `seon.agent/test-vars`; `seon.program/reaching-tests`, which disagreed with plan. Changes the code forced, done by worktree session PROVE (2026-10-22): (a) shards are processes of their own started by bin/test, not forks of the runner: a forked child of the jolt test runtime hangs at its first d/connect; (b) with a base, plan selects only the proofs and the moved digests (an agent's first turn would otherwise run main's whole suite); (c) the digest's inputs, merged ahead as jolt/reach-reuse and jolt/reach-inputs: quoted names by namespace (the digests followed the process's history), `seon.program/env-readers` (327 of 368 tests took the whole tree), a test's form reached only as its own root, system.edn's procs as data refs (`seon.system/declared-refs`), a test running the binary only through `seon.test-store/binary-sh` (a tree reader), and the config in force in :env (`test-runner/config-input`). bin/test builds target/seon whenever anything runs (a root on a binary older than its tree leaves agent vars unbound: task seon.program/loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound). Measured: docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches. One results store (root's ruling, option (a)): root reads its source checkout's inputs once through the :proof-inputs effect (`seon.proofs/perform-inputs`: the one `seon.proofs/inputs`, which bin/test calls too) and records them on main (`:seon.root/proof-inputs`); agents key by that fact, read greens from the shared directory and store their own runs' greens through root's :file owner; recomputed at boot and at `seon.root/apply!`, every other input changing only with a new root; a root with no source records none and its agents rerun. Open: shards are balanced by test count, not time (time never decides); seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes waits on an `nc -w 600` that never times out an idle connection on macOS."
